          "text": "**Observations:**\n- **Untrained border.** Every W1 row outside a sharp box keeps tiny values (|w| ≤ 0.036). That is the size of PyTorch's default initialisation (1/√784 ≈ 0.036), so those weights never moved.\n  - The trained weights (up to about ±0.6) start at input 116, which is row 4, column 4.\n  - Within each image row they run for 20 inputs (columns 4–23), followed by 8 untouched inputs.\n  - This repeats for image rows 4–23.\n  - So only the central 20×20 region ever contains non-zero pixels; there is a hard 4-pixel black frame.\n- **Content fills the box edges.** Pixels at the box's edges carry large weights. Examples: row 4 at columns 7–9 (about +0.55), and column 4 down rows 7–14 (+0.2 to +0.44). The objects reach the box boundary, especially left and right in the middle rows and at the top.\n- **A vertical centre stripe in the lower half.** In rows 15–22, columns 13–14 show one consistent feature:\n  - Units 8, 9 and 14 have strongly negative weights there (−0.2 to −0.33).\n  - Units 0, 2 and 6 have positive weights there.\n- **What that stripe does to class 1.** The output weights from all six of these units push in the same direction. A bright centre-bottom lowers the class-1 logit; a dark gap at the centre-bottom favours class 1.\n- **Other units.** They emphasise the left and right edges in the middle rows (sleeve-like regions) and the top rows.\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- **The hard box rules out raw MNIST, EMNIST and QMNIST.** These centre each digit by centre of mass, so a few pixels outside the central 20×20 are occasionally lit. Those border weights would then have trained away from their initial values.\n- **The centre stripe argues against digits.** In a digit dataset, a \"1\" is a bright stroke down the centre, so class 1 would be pushed up by centre-bottom brightness. Here it is the opposite.\n- **The stripe matches trousers.** \"Dark gap between two legs in the lower centre means class 1\" is the signature of Trouser in Fashion-MNIST.\n- **Other clothing cues fit too.** Edge activity in the middle rows matches sleeves of pullovers, coats and shirts. Top-row features match necklines and bag handles.\n- **The frame is unexplained.** Standard Fashion-MNIST fills the full 28×28 frame. This version seems to have been shrunk to 20×20 and padded with a 4-pixel border, a preprocessing step I can only infer.\n- **Natural-image sets do not fit.** Greyscale CIFAR-style images would give smoother, less class-specific weights and would show no such leg-gap feature.\n\n**Dataset:** Fashion-MNIST (Zalando clothing images, 28×28 greyscale). The object content appears to be confined to the central 20×20 region with a 4-pixel black border.\n\n**Classes:** 0 T-shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot.\n\n**Confidence:** 55%",
